Now it is my third Saturday at the café.
I have learned to recognize our regulars.
The first time I worked here, everything was completely new.
But now I have gotten used to the routines behind the counter.
Through the window I see an older man out on the street.
He walks slowly towards the front door.
When he was here last week, I noticed him.
He also came at exactly ten o'clock last week.
He is wearing the same brown coat as last time.
I already know what he would like to order.
I take a large cup from the top shelf.
Then I pour hot, black coffee into the cup.
I place it on the counter before he even opens the door.
The doorbell rings loudly as he steps in.
He walks straight over to me and looks surprised at the coffee.
"Is that for me?" he asks and smiles broadly.
"Yes, a large black coffee," I answer.
"That was very fast," he says and takes out his wallet.
"That will be twenty-eight kroner," I tell him.
It is a really nice feeling that I already know his routine.
